# shir_nodes/fs_same_file.node — the `-ef` file predicate.
#
# `[ A -ef B ]` / `[[ A -ef B ]]` / `test A -ef B` is true iff BOTH paths
# resolve to the same device and inode. Both operands must stat: two MISSING
# paths are NOT the same file (GNU test: "either operand missing ⇒ false"),
# and a path that fails to stat for any reason (ENOENT, EACCES on a directory
# component, ENOTDIR) makes the predicate false rather than an error.
#
# Node model: `same_file(left, right)` = exists(left) ∧ exists(right) ∧
# dev(left)=dev(right) ∧ ino(left)=ino(right). Symlinks are FOLLOWED: the
# predicate is defined over stat(2), not lstat(2), so a symlink and its target
# are the same file (measured on this box: `ln -s f l; [ f -ef l ]` is true).
#
# Out of model: filesystems that report inode 0 for every file (some FUSE,
# Windows) — the predicate then degrades to "same device", which is what the
# kernel does too, so no renderer may special-case it.
node FsSameFile
tag "FsSameFile"
kind expr
field left: expr
field right: expr
spec: FsSemantics#same_file
